Sliding Window Theory
=====================

Core Idea:
- A window (subarray/substring) slides across the data
- The window condition is monotonic -- you expand or shrink based on a clear rule
- You maintain state about the current window and update it incrementally


When Sliding Window Works:
--------------------------
- The answer lives inside a contiguous subarray/substring
- The window has a monotonic condition -- you can clearly decide when to expand or shrink
- No element outside the window affects elements inside it

Examples:
  - Longest substring without repeating characters
  - Maximum sum subarray of size k
  - Minimum window substring


When Sliding Window Does NOT Work:
-----------------------------------
- Removals change the structure of the string/array
- Elements merge after deletion, creating new conditions
- You need to look backward after processing forward
- The valid condition can appear at any point due to side effects of previous operations


Simple Test to Know:
--------------------
Ask yourself:
  "After I process/remove something, does the remaining data
   change in a way that affects what I already passed?"

  YES -> Sliding window won't work, think stack/deque
  NO  -> Sliding window is likely fine


Quick Reference:
----------------
Situation                                  |  Approach
------------------------------------------|--------------------
Fixed or variable window, no side effects  |  Sliding Window
Removals trigger chain reactions           |  Stack
Need previous context after removal        |  Stack
Partitioning or comparing                  |  Two Pointers
Monotonic queue needed                     |  Deque